To make environment variables available to EAS Build, they must be defined on EAS servers [1]. 

You can configure environment variables on your build profiles in **eas.json** using the `"env"` field, which will set them on the EAS Build builder [6]:

```json eas.json
{
  "build": {
    "production": {
      "env": {
        "API_URL": "https://company.com/api"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Additionally, you can set environment variables dynamically during job execution in build hooks using the `set-env` command [4]:

```sh
set-env EXAMPLE_ENV "example value"
```

After creating an environment variable, you can read it on subsequent EAS Build jobs with `process.env.VARIABLE_NAME` in Node.js or as `$VARIABLE_NAME` in shell scripts [4].

