To open a downloaded file in the platform's native preview using `expo-file-system`, use `File.preview()` after checking if the file can be previewed with `file.canPreview()` [4]. 

Here is an example:

```ts
import { File, Paths } from 'expo-file-system';

const file = await File.downloadFileAsync(
  'https://pdfobject.com/pdf/sample.pdf',
  new File(Paths.cache, 'sample.pdf')
);

if (await file.canPreview()) {
  await file.preview({ title: 'Sample PDF' });
}
```

File preview is supported on Android and iOS (presenting Quick Look on iOS and an `ACTION_VIEW` intent on Android) [4].

vs-4-v58.answer:
To set a large icon for Android notifications, use the `largeIcon` property in the `expo-notifications` config plugin within your app config (**app.json** or **app.config.js**) [1]. 

Here is an example:

```json app.json
{
  "expo": {
    "plugins": [
      [
        "expo-notifications",
        {
          "largeIcon": "./local/assets/notification_large_icon.png"
        }
      ]
    ]
  }
}
```

The `largeIcon` property takes a "Local path to an image to use as the large icon for notifications" [1]. The image is resized to 64x64 dp and displayed next to the notification text unless a notification carries its own image [1]. Because this requires building a new app binary, you will need to recompile your native Android app using EAS Build or `npx expo run:android` for the changes to take effect [1][3].

