rn-09.answer:
To determine whether a screen reader is active, use the `AccessibilityInfo` API [1][2]. You can query its current state using `AccessibilityInfo.isScreenReaderEnabled()` or listen for changes using `AccessibilityInfo.addEventListener('screenReaderChanged',...)` [2].

Here is an example of how to check and listen for screen reader status:

```tsx
import {useState, useEffect} from 'react';
import {AccessibilityInfo, Text, StyleSheet} from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  const [screenReaderEnabled, setScreenReaderEnabled] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const screenReaderChangedSubscription = AccessibilityInfo.addEventListener(
      'screenReaderChanged',
      isScreenReaderEnabled => {
        setScreenReaderEnabled(isScreenReaderEnabled);
      },
    );

    AccessibilityInfo.isScreenReaderEnabled().then(isScreenReaderEnabled => {
      setScreenReaderEnabled(isScreenReaderEnabled);
    });

    return () => {
      screenReaderChangedSubscription.remove();
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <Text style={styles.status}>
      The screen reader is {screenReaderEnabled? 'enabled': 'disabled'}.
    </Text>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  status: {
    margin: 30,
  },
});

export default App;
```
